00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.780
First, let's quickly run through the basics of what the point of sunscreen is. Sunscreen's main job is to

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:31.860
cut down how much UV reaches your skin.
Sunscreens have an SPF rating which

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.000
mostly measures how much UVB protection
you're getting. These shorter wavelengths

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.689
generally cause you to burn and they're
the wavelengths most strongly associated

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:43.770
with skin cancer. They don't go as deep
into your skin but they still cause your

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:48.600
skin to age faster. There's also UVA,
which are longer wavelengths. These burn

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:53.189
less but they cause prolonged tanning.
They contribute to melanoma, skin aging,

00:01:53.189 --> 00:01:57.840
and hyperpigmentation. The broad spectrum
or UVA ratings on sunscreens tell you

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.619
how much UVA protection you're getting.
One of the problems with this is most of

00:02:01.619 --> 00:02:05.759
the studies done on UV have been done on
people with white skin- we'll come back

00:02:05.759 --> 00:02:09.060
to this point later. Some people have
also asked about light from screens,

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.709
particularly blue light. Sunscreen will
not protect you against blue light, not

00:02:13.709 --> 00:02:17.820
even mineral sunscreens. Your screens
also don't produce anywhere near enough

00:02:17.820 --> 00:02:21.000
blue light to make a difference. If you
want to know more about blue light I've

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:25.320
done a couple of videos on that, I'll
link them below. So back to UV. UV is bad

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.130
so sunscreen is good, but there are two
big variables that change whether or not

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:31.380
wearing sunscreen is good for your
situation:

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:39.160
how much UV you're exposed to, and how
susceptible your skin is to UV damage.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:44.110
First let's talk about how much UV
you're exposed to when you're inside.

00:02:44.110 --> 00:02:48.430
There are two types of ways you can be
exposed to UV from the sun. Direct

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:52.300
exposure is when the sun is shining
directly on you. When you're in the sun

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:55.780
you have direct exposure, when you're in
the shade you have no direct exposure.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.670
Diffuse exposure is when the sun isn't
shining directly on you. Basically the

00:03:00.670 --> 00:03:04.900
sun's UV is bounced onto you by either
air molecules high up in the sky or

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:09.250
objects around you. This also happens
with visible light and that's why you

00:03:09.250 --> 00:03:13.180
can see objects that are in the shade.
Shorter wavelengths get diffused more, so

00:03:13.180 --> 00:03:17.440
there's more diffuse UV around then
there is diffuse visible light. Obviously

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:21.190
there's more of both types of UV when
you're outside compared to inside, that's

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:24.850
because when you're indoors there are
things in between you and the sun and

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:28.540
the sky. Firstly there are solid walls
that block out direct UV if you're in

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:33.459
the shade, but when you're in the shade
you can still get diffuse UV. So how can

00:03:33.459 --> 00:03:37.060
we estimate how much diffuse UV we're
getting? This is actually quite tricky.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:41.739
There are lots of different factors that
change the ratio of direct to diffuse UV;

00:03:41.740 --> 00:03:47.050
things like height of the sun, wavelength,
altitude... I read a bunch of papers and I

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.520
got really lost in the calculations.

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.320
*Michelle hearing her own voice overlapping on itself*

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:56.800
*THE VOICES GET LOUDER*

00:03:56.800 --> 00:03:58.600
*unsettling music*

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:03.320
I had a great time researching this
video. I'm going to use 60% diffuse,

00:04:03.350 --> 00:04:08.120
40% direct as a rough estimate on the higher
end of the scale. To estimate how much

00:04:08.120 --> 00:04:12.110
diffuse UV you're getting you can use
something called sky view. Basically if

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:15.560
you're standing in the middle of the
field you're exposed to the inside of a

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:20.750
dome of sky. That's 100 percent exposure
to diffuse UV. If there's stuff blocking

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.220
your view of the sky; trees, buildings,
walls... then you get less sky view and

00:04:25.220 --> 00:04:29.780
less diffuse UV. Uhat's why the distance
from the window matters. My window is

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:34.200
about 1.6 by 1.2 metres. To work out my
sky view...

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:42.240
*EMOTIVE VIOLIN AND PIANO MUSIC*

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:46.280
Since it's around head height I'm going
to approximate it as a rectangle.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:49.790
The further away you are from the window, the
more accurate this is. If you're sitting

00:04:49.790 --> 00:04:53.960
in the window and there's no trees or
buildings, then you're getting about 50%

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.700
sky view, so roughly 50% of the diffuse
UV. If you're further back from the

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:03.290
window, then the portion of your dome
that sky is going to be smaller. You can

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:08.030
see that it decreases exponentially. For
example in my room I sit three metres

00:05:08.030 --> 00:05:12.260
away from the window so I get no direct
UV and I would get about 3%

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:17.510
diffuse UV from the sky. But I also have
trees, houses, blinds blocking up parts of

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:23.120
the sky, so only about 11% of the sky in
the window is actually visible. The UV

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:27.560
reflected by these objects is usually
less than 10% so I'm ignoring it. That

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:31.460
gives me 0.33% sky view, which means I'm getting

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:36.200
one three hundredths of the diffuse UV
compared to being in the open and one

00:05:36.200 --> 00:05:40.910
five hundredths of the total UV compared
to being in the open. Even if I was only

00:05:40.910 --> 00:05:44.630
one meter away from the window, that's
less than two percent sky view. I'm

00:05:44.630 --> 00:05:48.680
getting about one percent of the amount
of total UV compared to being in the

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:53.980
open. But there's usually another thing
between us and the sun and that is glass,

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:58.670
Normal window glass usually blocks all
of the UVB from getting through but only

00:05:58.670 --> 00:06:03.950
about 1/4 to 1/3 of UVA so in my
particular situation I'm going to get

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:08.420
none of the UVB and only one five
hundredth of the UVA compared to sitting

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:14.160
outside in the open. but the amount of UV
that you get out in the open also changes.

00:06:17.350 --> 00:06:22.250
UV levels depend on where you live, the
time of day, and the time of year.

00:06:22.250 --> 00:06:26.270
You might have heard of the UV index: this
tells you how much erythemal or sunburn

00:06:26.270 --> 00:06:29.930
causing UV you're getting in a
particular location at a particular time.

00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:34.160
You can look this up for your city. In
Sydney for example, you get a maximum of

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.390
12 in the middle of summer, and a max of
2 in the middle of winter. You can use

00:06:38.390 --> 00:06:43.640
this to get an estimate of your UVA
levels. There is a myth that UVA levels

00:06:43.640 --> 00:06:48.320
don't change much throughout the day or
throughout the year. That is a myth, they

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:52.640
do, they just don't change quite as much
as UVB does. You can see on these graphs

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:56.990
that UVA, the red line, still peaks in the
middle of the day and in the middle of

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:01.100
summer. The UV index is the best way to
work out how much UV you have in your

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:05.180
area. So once you work out roughly how
much UV you're getting, you can work out

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:13.580
what's best for your situation. Not
everyone needs to wear sunscreen every

00:07:13.580 --> 00:07:17.240
day. I know it's a common saying in
skincare circles, that you need to wear

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:22.190
sunscreen every day, but this is not true
for everyone. There are benefits to UV

00:07:22.190 --> 00:07:26.810
exposure which I talked about in my SPF
myths video. Our body produces vitamin D

00:07:26.810 --> 00:07:31.610
after UVB exposure and nitric oxide
after UVA exposure so in many countries

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:35.990
including the UK and Australia, which is
the skin cancer capital of the world,

00:07:35.990 --> 00:07:39.290
health authorities actually don't
recommend that you wear sunscreen every

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:42.980
day even if you go outside. The
Australian guidelines based this on the

00:07:42.980 --> 00:07:47.750
UV index. If the maximum UV on a
particular day is 3 or above they

00:07:47.750 --> 00:07:52.040
recommend wearing sunscreen but if it's
below 3 they recommend not wearing

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:55.820
sunscreen and intentionally trying to
get some sun exposure. You don't get much

00:07:55.820 --> 00:07:59.960
UVB through glass so the vitamin D
benefits aren't there,but it isn't a

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:03.110
blanket rule that you have to wear
sunscreen every day. There's also a

00:08:03.110 --> 00:08:07.340
racial bias in sunscreen studies. Like in
pretty much every other area of health,

00:08:07.340 --> 00:08:11.870
the vast majority of studies have been
done on white people, who are melanin

00:08:11.870 --> 00:08:16.430
deprived. Melanin is skin pigment which
acts as a natural sunscreen: not really

00:08:16.430 --> 00:08:20.150
enough to hang out on the beach without
sunscreen, but enough to protect you from

00:08:20.150 --> 00:08:23.990
some incidental exposure so the benefits
of wearing sunscreen for preventing

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:27.410
cancer and skin-aging 
is going to be lower for people with

00:08:27.410 --> 00:08:31.850
darker skin. On the flipside UVA does
tend to cause more pigmentation in dark

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:35.660
skin. This is more of an aesthetic thing
than a serious health issue so whether

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:39.530
or not you still need UVA protection
indoors really depends on how much you

00:08:39.530 --> 00:08:43.520
care about pigmentation. You might also
be using skin care that increases your

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.660
sensitivity to UV like alpha hydroxy
acids and if you're using expensive

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:52.100
antiaging skincare then it might be
worth protecting yourself to maximize

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.490
your results. You might also have a
special condition that means you have to

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:59.810
care a lot more about UV exposure like
if you have a photosensitive disease, or

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:04.400
a family history of skin cancer. So all
of these factors are going to influence

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:06.920
your choice.
There are also some downsides to

00:09:06.920 --> 00:09:11.030
sunscreen, the obvious one is cost. You
also have to spend time and effort

00:09:11.030 --> 00:09:15.350
applying the sunscreen, and in a lot of
people like me, wearing sunscreen can

00:09:15.350 --> 00:09:19.460
cause clogged pores. And there are also
health concerns about wearing sunscreen.

00:09:19.460 --> 00:09:23.420
These health concerns are usually really
small, if you're going outside the

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:27.770
benefits greatly outweigh the risks, but
if you're exposed to only a tiny amount

00:09:27.770 --> 00:09:31.940
of UV then these health concerns
combined with all of the other drawbacks

00:09:31.940 --> 00:09:35.900
of wearing sunscreen might tip the
scales towards you not wearing sunscreen.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:39.920
There's actually a really well-known
example that illustrates the difference

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:44.330
between diffuse indoor and direct indoor
exposure. It's this truck driver who

00:09:44.330 --> 00:09:49.250
drove for 28 years with the windows
closed. Sun struck this side directly but

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:52.880
the other side only got diffuse exposure
through the side windows which are

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.770
similar to window glass. There is a huge
difference between the two sides. I think

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:02.060
it goes to show that direct UVA exposure
indoors is probably worth worrying about

00:10:02.060 --> 00:10:06.380
but diffuse indoor UVA really depends on
your situation.

